  "vitDGuideTitle": "Sun & vitamin D guide",
  "vitDGuideBenefitsTitle": "Benefits",
  "vitDGuideBenefits": "Vitamin D supports strong bones and teeth, muscle strength, and the immune system. Low vitamin D is common among monastics who stay indoors, keep the robe covered, or live in temples with deep shade. A little regular sun is a natural and free way to make it.",
  "vitDGuideNoonTitle": "Short noon session vs. long afternoon session",
  "vitDGuideNoon": "A short session near midday is better than a long one in the late afternoon. When the sun is high (above 45°) the light contains more UVB, the rays that make vitamin D, so you make the most vitamin D for each unit of sunburn risk. At 3 pm the sun is lower: UVB drops much faster than UVA. You burn more slowly, but to get the same vitamin D you must stay far longer, and you receive more UVA, which ages the skin and adds to skin cancer risk. So a longer 3 pm session is not safer — it is less efficient. Example: 10–15 minutes at noon can equal an hour or more at 3 pm.",
  "vitDGuideShortTitle": "Short, regular sessions",
  "vitDGuideShort": "The skin's vitamin D production levels off: after roughly half to one burn dose, more time adds little vitamin D but keeps adding skin damage. Several short sessions during the week are better than one long session. Stopping well before your skin turns pink is enough. Vitamin D is stored in the body, so a missed day is not a problem.",
  "vitDGuideDangersTitle": "Dangers",
  "vitDGuideDangers": "Too much sun causes sunburn, eye damage, heat exhaustion and dehydration, skin ageing, and skin cancer. Fair skin burns quickly. Glass windows block UVB, so sitting behind glass makes no vitamin D. Water, sand and pale stone reflect UV and raise exposure. Some medicines make the skin sensitive to light. Drink water, and protect your eyes — never look at the sun.",
  "vitDGuideMonksTitle": "Tips for the Sangha",
  "vitDGuideMonks": "Uncovering the right shoulder or the arms while sitting or walking in the monastery for a short time around midday is usually enough for darker skin types in the tropics. A shaved head is also exposed, so protect the scalp on long alms rounds. Darker skin needs longer for the same vitamin D but burns less easily. In cold climates or seasons when the sun stays below 45°, the body cannot make much vitamin D — ask a doctor about food or supplements.",
  "vitDGuideMethodTitle": "How this is estimated",
  "vitDGuideMethod": "Sun angle comes from your GPS location and the time. UV index is estimated for a clear sky (UVI ≈ 12.5 × sin(angle)^2.42) and reduced for clouds. Burn dose (MED) depends on skin type. About 10,000 IU is made when the whole body receives one burn dose; this is scaled by the uncovered skin area and your body size (from weight). Vitamin D efficiency falls when the sun is below 45° and is zero below 15°. Production stops as the day's dose reaches one burn dose. Real values vary with ozone, altitude, air pollution, age and health — treat these numbers as rough guides.",
  "vitDDisclaimer": "Medical disclaimer: this calculator gives rough educational estimates only. It is not medical advice and cannot measure your vitamin D level. Sun exposure carries risks, including sunburn and skin cancer. Consult a qualified doctor about vitamin D testing, supplements, or skin concerns, especially if you have fair skin, a history of skin cancer, or take light-sensitising medicine.",
