  "vitDGuideMethod2": "Posture and scalp: the estimate is calibrated for an upright person. Lying flat turns the exposed side to the sky, so at midday it makes up to about 1.6× more vitamin D, but less than standing when the sun is low. Sunburn time does not change, because the most exposed skin always faces the sky. For monastics a shaved scalp (about 3.5% of the body) is counted, and regrowth shades it gradually over two weeks. Target time follows the sun's path from now and includes earlier sessions today.",
  "vitDTaperNote": "Vitamin D production slows as your skin's sun dose builds up, so each extra minute gives a little less.",
  "vitDGuideBestTitle": "Best method: more skin, short time",
  "vitDGuideBest": "Burning depends on how long each patch of skin is in the sun; vitamin D depends on how much skin is out. So uncover as much skin as you can for a short time near midday. Lying down turns more skin toward the high sun. Spend half the time on your back and half on your front: each side gets only half the burn dose while the vitamin D is about the same. Cover the face and head with a cloth, close your eyes, and never fall asleep in the sun. For fair skin at tropical noon, 3–5 minutes like this is often enough; darker skin may need 5–10 minutes.",
  "vitDGuideBurnTitle": "About sunburn",
  "vitDGuideBurn": "A sunburn is the skin's reaction to UVB damage to its cells. Redness appears 2–6 hours later and is worst after 12–24 hours, so you cannot feel it while you are in the sun. Sessions on the same day add up: four 5-minute sessions burn about as much as one 20-minute session, because the skin repairs slowly over many hours. The meter in this app adds all of today's sessions and resets at midnight. Staying below half a burn dose (50%) avoids pinkness. The burn risk mostly resets each day, but skin ageing and skin cancer risk build up over a lifetime, so short regular sessions are better than long ones.",
  "vitDTabLayFemale": "Woman",
  "vitDCoverSwimwearFemaleDesc": "Swim top and shorts",
  "vitDCoverAngsa": "Shoulder cloth (aṅsa)",
  "vitDCoverAngsaDesc": "Right arm, left forearm, right chest bare",
  "vitDSessionRunning": "IN THE SUN",
  "vitDSessionIdle": "READY",
  "vitDBurnInfoTitle": "About the sunburn dose",
  "vitDBurnInfo": "The sunburn dose is cumulative: it adds up all of today's sessions. Four 5-minute sessions count the same as one 20-minute session, because the skin repairs slowly over many hours. It resets at midnight.\n\n100% means your skin will probably turn pink a few hours later. Staying under 50% avoids this.\n\nFor vitamin D, the best way is more skin for less time. Uncover as much skin as you can near midday and keep the session short.\n\nLie down: spend half the time face up, with your face and head covered by a cloth, and half face down. Each side gets only half the burn dose, while the vitamin D stays about the same.",
